00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:43.890
is a recent blog post that
was talking a little bit more

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:46.090
about committed use discounts.

00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:48.540
So if you're not familiar
with what they are,

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:51.480
they're basically a way of
setting up a reservation

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:54.060
and saying, I'm going to use
a certain amount of cores

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:57.900
or memory or local
SSD or even GPUs

00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:00.157
over the next one
year or three years.

00:02:00.157 --> 00:02:02.490
And when you do that, you end
up getting those resources

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:04.090
for a little bit cheaper.

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:06.655
So if you've got these
large static workloads,

00:02:06.655 --> 00:02:08.530
then it might make a
lot of sense to say, OK.

00:02:08.530 --> 00:02:10.800
Well, I know I'm going to
be running a medium CPU,

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:13.210
maybe two CPUs
over the next year.

00:02:13.210 --> 00:02:16.330
So why don't I go ahead and
pay a little bit less for them?

00:02:16.330 --> 00:02:18.570
And then there's
also now a report

00:02:18.570 --> 00:02:21.030
that you can actually look
at an interactive dashboard

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:25.023
that kind of shows you where
your commits are being used.

00:02:25.023 --> 00:02:26.940
And you can look at it
per project, per region

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:27.730
and say, OK.

00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:30.060
Well, this is a good
candidate for me

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:32.190
to set up a committed
use discount here,

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:35.197
because I've got all these
cores that I'm statically using.

00:02:35.197 --> 00:02:36.780
So I find that report
really exciting.

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:38.850
And I think a lot of
people can use that.

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:41.510
