00:09:42.330 --> 00:09:45.030
We took care of all
the client's needs,

00:09:45.030 --> 00:09:51.180
including branding, logo design,
motion graphics, web designs,

00:09:51.180 --> 00:09:52.130
all sorts of things.

00:09:52.130 --> 00:09:55.710
It was really good for me to
just get a breadth of work

00:09:55.710 --> 00:09:59.070
in graphic design and
just design in general.

00:09:59.070 --> 00:10:01.830
And that's at that
time, it was starting

00:10:01.830 --> 00:10:06.720
to become a more defined role,
like interaction designer.

00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:09.960
And so I moved at
that point to Seattle

00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:11.650
and started working
for Microsoft.

00:10:11.650 --> 00:10:14.130
And there were actual
interaction designers there.

00:10:14.130 --> 00:10:17.280
And so I thought, well, this
is falling from heaven, almost.

00:10:17.280 --> 00:10:19.620
Everything that I've
liked in the design

00:10:19.620 --> 00:10:22.050
world so far sort
of all families

00:10:22.050 --> 00:10:25.680
up into this amazing role
of becoming an interaction

00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:28.530
designer, thinking
about everything

00:10:28.530 --> 00:10:31.920
end to end, from
visuals to audio

00:10:31.920 --> 00:10:36.660
to flows and mental models
and psychology and research.

00:10:36.660 --> 00:10:38.410
And I just fell in love with it.

00:10:38.410 --> 00:10:42.833
And so I ended up following
that path to what I do today.

00:10:42.833 --> 00:10:45.000
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So this
is a question mostly borne

00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:46.620
of my own ignorance.

00:10:46.620 --> 00:10:50.070
But how come nobody
appreciates interaction design?

00:10:50.070 --> 00:10:53.040
And is that correct, because
I kind of get the feeling

00:10:53.040 --> 00:10:57.323
that a lot of people don't think
about it first and foremost

00:10:57.323 --> 00:10:59.490
and, in fact, don't think
about it at all sometimes.

00:10:59.490 --> 00:11:02.030
GABI FERRARA: But isn't that
a result of good design,

00:11:02.030 --> 00:11:03.968
if you don't think
about it as a user?

00:11:03.968 --> 00:11:05.010
MICHAEL KLEINERMAN: Yeah.

00:11:05.010 --> 00:11:05.980
I think that's right.

00:11:05.980 --> 00:11:10.500
If the interface is invisible
and the interaction designer

00:11:10.500 --> 00:11:13.260
has worked on that so hard
to just really get out

00:11:13.260 --> 00:11:16.020
of the way, then yeah, you
should strive for that.

00:11:16.020 --> 00:11:18.885
But yeah, I guess that's the
other angle of that question.

00:11:18.885 --> 00:11:20.010
For example-- I don't know.

00:11:20.010 --> 00:11:22.120
If you have a startup,
you're thinking,

00:11:22.120 --> 00:11:25.630
oh, we need designers
because it's the trend.

00:11:25.630 --> 00:11:27.070
We just see it everywhere.

00:11:27.070 --> 00:11:29.550
And you just happen to
hire visual designers,

00:11:29.550 --> 00:11:32.490
because you think that's
all it's encompassing.

00:11:32.490 --> 00:11:34.740
I've seen a lot of
scenarios like that.

00:11:34.740 --> 00:11:38.010
Yeah, you end up
having some gaps.

00:11:38.010 --> 00:11:39.930
I've known a lot
of visual designers

00:11:39.930 --> 00:11:43.840
that end up understanding
so much of everything.

00:11:43.840 --> 00:11:46.500
And they know how to
prototype, and their skills

00:11:46.500 --> 00:11:47.430
are just amazing.

00:11:47.430 --> 00:11:52.140
But some are just kind of
coming out of a background that

00:11:52.140 --> 00:11:53.770
is just very focused on that.

00:11:53.770 --> 00:11:57.120
And so you can't really expect
to have a visual designer

00:11:57.120 --> 00:12:00.570
think about all the other
aspects of all the other design

00:12:00.570 --> 00:12:01.690
disciplines.

00:12:01.690 --> 00:12:02.980
So I agree with you.

00:12:02.980 --> 00:12:06.030
Sometimes, it's easy to think
that the disciplines are all

00:12:06.030 --> 00:12:06.990
the same.

00:12:06.990 --> 00:12:10.020
But no, this is why you
have a specialized motion

00:12:10.020 --> 00:12:13.380
designer, a sound designer,
an interaction designer,

00:12:13.380 --> 00:12:15.070
a visual designer, and so forth.

00:12:15.070 --> 00:12:17.730
GABI FERRARA: So how does
designing systems at Google

00:12:17.730 --> 00:12:19.020
work for you?

00:12:19.020 --> 00:12:20.448
How is this process?

00:12:20.448 --> 00:12:22.740
MICHAEL KLEINERMAN: So I
guess there are two audiences.

00:12:22.740 --> 00:12:26.310
For me, who I'm trying to
build a design system on top

00:12:26.310 --> 00:12:30.000
of material design, I'm
having to sort of build

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:33.990
a context between
what Google uses

00:12:33.990 --> 00:12:36.840
and then what products that
are built on top of Google

00:12:36.840 --> 00:12:38.010
are using.

00:12:38.010 --> 00:12:40.500
And there's a
designer that actually

00:12:40.500 --> 00:12:45.060
consumes what we currently
have under Google Material.

00:12:45.060 --> 00:12:47.670
And they just go to a
website like material.io,

00:12:47.670 --> 00:12:51.570
which is where you just
find guidelines for all

00:12:51.570 --> 00:12:53.440
the patterns or the components.

00:12:53.440 --> 00:12:56.350
But in my case, when I
joined Cloud Firestore,

00:12:56.350 --> 00:13:00.570
what I had to do was
adopt material design

00:13:00.570 --> 00:13:01.900
for the first time.

00:13:01.900 --> 00:13:05.760
Nobody in cloud had
any parts of the UI

00:13:05.760 --> 00:13:08.520
in the material design language.

00:13:08.520 --> 00:13:12.570
It was still I think called
"Kennedy" before they

00:13:12.570 --> 00:13:14.280
launched the material design.

00:13:14.280 --> 00:13:17.850
So it was really tough, because
it was the very first product

00:13:17.850 --> 00:13:19.560
that was trying to do that.

00:13:19.560 --> 00:13:23.310
And it was still catching
up, because at that point,

00:13:23.310 --> 00:13:26.770
they were launching already the
second generation of material

00:13:26.770 --> 00:13:27.270
design.

00:13:27.270 --> 00:13:29.602
And we were just trying to
start with the first one.

00:13:29.602 --> 00:13:31.560
MARK MIRCHANDANI: And to
take a step back here,

00:13:31.560 --> 00:13:33.300
could you define
"material design"?

00:13:33.300 --> 00:13:33.570
MICHAEL KLEINERMAN: Yeah.

00:13:33.570 --> 00:13:34.380
Sure.

00:13:34.380 --> 00:13:38.790
So material design is kind of
the first design system that

00:13:38.790 --> 00:13:43.680
was driven by Google to
understand how the components

00:13:43.680 --> 00:13:47.490
and patterns work across
different types of devices

00:13:47.490 --> 00:13:49.930
for different screen sizes.

00:13:49.930 --> 00:13:52.950
So there were other
design systems out there,

00:13:52.950 --> 00:13:54.720
like Bootstrap,
things like that,

00:13:54.720 --> 00:13:57.930
where you have a
library of widgets

00:13:57.930 --> 00:14:00.750
or a library of components where
a designer or developer can

00:14:00.750 --> 00:14:04.980
just start using as boilerplate
to create any website.

00:14:04.980 --> 00:14:07.920
But nobody had
really thought, OK.

00:14:07.920 --> 00:14:11.220
Now, we're looking
at watches and phones

00:14:11.220 --> 00:14:13.020
and TVs and everything.

00:14:13.020 --> 00:14:16.050
And how would a design
system that spans

00:14:16.050 --> 00:14:19.960
across multi-device
platforms would work?

00:14:19.960 --> 00:14:21.600
And I think that's
how it started.

00:14:21.600 --> 00:14:22.080
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Gotcha.

00:14:22.080 --> 00:14:23.590
So you were talking
about how you

00:14:23.590 --> 00:14:25.560
were working with the
Cloud team in helping

00:14:25.560 --> 00:14:27.180
them implement design.

00:14:27.180 --> 00:14:29.435
But they hadn't really
caught up to material design.

00:14:29.435 --> 00:14:30.810
MICHAEL KLEINERMAN:
That's right,

00:14:30.810 --> 00:14:33.510
specifically talking of
the Google Cloud console.

00:14:33.510 --> 00:14:35.010
Firestore-- I don't
know if you guys

00:14:35.010 --> 00:14:36.180
are familiar with Firestore.

00:14:36.180 --> 00:14:42.810
But it lives in both
consoles, Firebase and Cloud.

00:14:42.810 --> 00:14:46.560
So there are completely
different design languages.

00:14:46.560 --> 00:14:49.770
Firebase, I think they
were a little bit ahead.

00:14:49.770 --> 00:14:52.830
They were already
adopting material design.

00:14:52.830 --> 00:14:54.960
The roundness of the
buttons, they already

00:14:54.960 --> 00:14:56.700
looked like material design.

00:14:56.700 --> 00:14:58.783
And the negative
space, the white space

00:14:58.783 --> 00:15:00.450
that you would see
on every single page,

00:15:00.450 --> 00:15:04.140
was already speaking
that design language.

00:15:04.140 --> 00:15:08.380
And on Google Cloud, we
just hadn't gotten there.

00:15:08.380 --> 00:15:10.830
And so that was kind
of the first thing

00:15:10.830 --> 00:15:15.090
that I did as I was building
the UX for Firestore.

00:15:15.090 --> 00:15:16.890
I was also trying
to figure out, how

00:15:16.890 --> 00:15:21.210
do we just start implementing
all these new components that

00:15:21.210 --> 00:15:22.830
are coming from material design?

00:15:22.830 --> 00:15:26.400
GABI FERRARA: So all
of these creative ways

00:15:26.400 --> 00:15:29.940
you do to make UX
design for the user,

00:15:29.940 --> 00:15:32.250
how do you reach all
those conclusions?

00:15:32.250 --> 00:15:34.290
Is that a design process?

00:15:34.290 --> 00:15:35.610
Is there research?

00:15:35.610 --> 00:15:36.962
What do you do?

00:15:36.962 --> 00:15:39.420
MICHAEL KLEINERMAN: So you mean
for implementing the design

00:15:39.420 --> 00:15:39.960
system?

00:15:39.960 --> 00:15:40.770
GABI FERRARA: Yeah.

00:15:40.770 --> 00:15:44.190
You do a decision, like, let's
say, oh, this menu should not

00:15:44.190 --> 00:15:47.700
be this size
because x, y, and z.

00:15:47.700 --> 00:15:50.640
And research shows that
this would be worse.

00:15:50.640 --> 00:15:51.620
I don't know.

00:15:51.620 --> 00:15:53.200
Decisions need to be made.

00:15:53.200 --> 00:15:55.428
And what do you base
those decisions on?

00:15:55.428 --> 00:15:56.470
MICHAEL KLEINERMAN: Yeah.

00:15:56.470 --> 00:15:59.580
So I think at the sort of
foundational level, which

00:15:59.580 --> 00:16:02.640
is what we get from
material design team,

00:16:02.640 --> 00:16:04.620
they had to go
through a lot of that.

00:16:04.620 --> 00:16:07.540
They had to go through a
lot of testing and research

00:16:07.540 --> 00:16:12.420
to figure out what is the right
size for a button or the tap

00:16:12.420 --> 00:16:16.260
target for making it work
on all different devices--

00:16:16.260 --> 00:16:18.788
contrast for accessibility.

00:16:18.788 --> 00:16:20.580
I guess those are all
the requirements that

00:16:20.580 --> 00:16:22.510
have to go for
that design system

00:16:22.510 --> 00:16:26.320
to sort of be published for
everyone to start using it.

00:16:26.320 --> 00:16:27.360
The same goes for Cloud.

00:16:27.360 --> 00:16:32.700
So what's been happening is that
the initial design system that

00:16:32.700 --> 00:16:35.400
was composed under
material design

00:16:35.400 --> 00:16:38.970
was almost focused
on consumer products.

00:16:38.970 --> 00:16:43.200
And Cloud starts reaching
this new space, which

00:16:43.200 --> 00:16:45.923
is called "Enterprise,"
where you are thinking

00:16:45.923 --> 00:16:48.090
about components that are
a little bit more complex,

00:16:48.090 --> 00:16:51.690
like tables that need
to account for millions

00:16:51.690 --> 00:16:55.575
and millions of columns, with
raw data coming in super fast

00:16:55.575 --> 00:16:56.450
and things like that.

00:16:56.450 --> 00:17:01.690
And that was not part of
the library of components.

00:17:01.690 --> 00:17:04.859
So we do the same
sort of process.

00:17:04.859 --> 00:17:08.400
We test the functionality
of every single element

00:17:08.400 --> 00:17:11.339
in that table, the
accessibility of it,

00:17:11.339 --> 00:17:13.810
until it reaches all
the requirements.

00:17:13.810 --> 00:17:16.224
And then we can say, this is
ready for people to use it.

00:17:16.224 --> 00:17:18.599
MARK MIRCHANDANI: You kind of
mentioned a little bit just

00:17:18.599 --> 00:17:20.380
earlier about accessibility.

00:17:20.380 --> 00:17:22.540
Google has some
pretty tremendous

00:17:22.540 --> 00:17:26.230
accessibility guidelines
built into everything we do.

00:17:26.230 --> 00:17:27.940
How does that kind
of play together

00:17:27.940 --> 00:17:33.820
with not just the visual design,
but the instructional design,

00:17:33.820 --> 00:17:35.200
all the design?

00:17:35.200 --> 00:17:37.090
Everything you're
building, you also

00:17:37.090 --> 00:17:38.950
need to keep
accessibility in mind.

00:17:38.950 --> 00:17:40.870
How do those kind of
interplay together?

00:17:40.870 --> 00:17:41.912
MICHAEL KLEINERMAN: Yeah.

00:17:41.912 --> 00:17:44.350
So it's a super top
priority for Cloud.

00:17:44.350 --> 00:17:48.070
And Google has different
sort of requirement levels

00:17:48.070 --> 00:17:50.170
that you also might
need to decide

00:17:50.170 --> 00:17:52.600
as a team which one
are we shooting for,

00:17:52.600 --> 00:17:55.420
at least this year
or this quarter.

00:17:55.420 --> 00:17:59.530
And basically, I think they
fall into primary use cases.

00:17:59.530 --> 00:18:02.050
In your product,
you always know what

00:18:02.050 --> 00:18:05.800
are the top three
sort of flows or tasks

00:18:05.800 --> 00:18:10.860
that a user comes to your
product to solve or to do.

00:18:10.860 --> 00:18:15.100
And focusing on those main ones
could get you to, I don't know,

00:18:15.100 --> 00:18:19.060
the first or the second level
of accessibility requirements.

00:18:19.060 --> 00:18:22.540
Now, if you keep fixing
accessibility issues

00:18:22.540 --> 00:18:26.050
for the secondary
use cases, then you

00:18:26.050 --> 00:18:29.320
can get to the next level.

00:18:29.320 --> 00:18:31.990
And we're actually
now thinking, what

00:18:31.990 --> 00:18:35.890
happens beyond that last
level, which is really

00:18:35.890 --> 00:18:40.090
going through a screen reader,
for example, experience,

00:18:40.090 --> 00:18:43.120
where you can actually
accomplish everything you do.

00:18:43.120 --> 00:18:46.900
But it's not a nice
experience, not as delightful

00:18:46.900 --> 00:18:50.260
as, I don't know, what you would
see on YouTube when you upload

00:18:50.260 --> 00:18:54.460
a video or something like
that-- so really focusing on,

00:18:54.460 --> 00:18:57.400
how do you really make the
experience better for people

00:18:57.400 --> 00:18:59.360
with any disability?

00:18:59.360 --> 00:19:02.500
And I guess the other aspect
is from the design system

00:19:02.500 --> 00:19:04.870
point of view, how do you
make sure that there's

00:19:04.870 --> 00:19:07.690
a lot of guidance also for
people that are consuming

00:19:07.690 --> 00:19:09.820
the components and the
patterns that you have

00:19:09.820 --> 00:19:12.460
in the design system website?

00:19:12.460 --> 00:19:15.820
So not only at the component
level, but at the page level--

00:19:15.820 --> 00:19:19.420
we already know people come
to the cloud design system

00:19:19.420 --> 00:19:24.370
to create some sort of page
that lists all the resources,

00:19:24.370 --> 00:19:26.530
resources being all
your virtual machines

00:19:26.530 --> 00:19:29.880
or all your buckets from
storage or databases or data

00:19:29.880 --> 00:19:32.232
sets from machine
learning models.

00:19:32.232 --> 00:19:33.440
It doesn't matter what it is.

00:19:33.440 --> 00:19:35.440
We kind of know that
that's a pattern,

00:19:35.440 --> 00:19:37.960
and we provide guidance
for accessibility,

00:19:37.960 --> 00:19:39.585
how to traverse
through the whole page.

00:19:39.585 --> 00:19:41.543
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So
that's really interesting,

00:19:41.543 --> 00:19:44.500
because you were just mentioning
that even inside of Cloud,

00:19:44.500 --> 00:19:47.308
Firebase had already
kind of adopted

00:19:47.308 --> 00:19:49.600
a lot of these guidelines
and was at a different place,

00:19:49.600 --> 00:19:52.270
even though Firebase
is under Cloud.

00:19:52.270 --> 00:19:53.970
And you were also
mentioning earlier

00:19:53.970 --> 00:19:56.590
that you worked on the YouTube
team and the Android team.

00:19:56.590 --> 00:19:58.823
So these are all
different guidelines,

00:19:58.823 --> 00:20:00.490
and they're at different
places in terms

00:20:00.490 --> 00:20:01.548
of the material design.

00:20:01.548 --> 00:20:03.340
What do they do the
same, and what are they

00:20:03.340 --> 00:20:04.120
doing differently?

00:20:04.120 --> 00:20:06.850
And why aren't they all
kind of at the same place?

00:20:06.850 --> 00:20:07.892
MICHAEL KLEINERMAN: Yeah.

00:20:07.892 --> 00:20:11.260
So with Firebase, it's
a very unique case.

00:20:11.260 --> 00:20:13.870
And I think it does have
a similarity to YouTube,

00:20:13.870 --> 00:20:16.240
in the sense that it's
all part of Google.

00:20:16.240 --> 00:20:18.060
But they have their own brand.

00:20:18.060 --> 00:20:21.670
So they're not really
consuming material design

00:20:21.670 --> 00:20:23.890
or Google material
design exactly

00:20:23.890 --> 00:20:28.600
as a Google product, a product
that is branded by Google.

00:20:28.600 --> 00:20:31.570
So YouTube and Firebase
are these two snowflakes.

00:20:31.570 --> 00:20:33.250
And what they've
done is basically,

00:20:33.250 --> 00:20:37.030
they break a little bit of
the style of the entire design

00:20:37.030 --> 00:20:38.770
system to fit their brand.

00:20:38.770 --> 00:20:39.520
And it works.

00:20:39.520 --> 00:20:42.670
In the case of
Firebase, their audience

00:20:42.670 --> 00:20:46.450
has always been almost
entry-level developers or even

00:20:46.450 --> 00:20:47.610
startups.

00:20:47.610 --> 00:20:51.640
What they want is just to
make it super easy to just

00:20:51.640 --> 00:20:53.560
get started with
an app, an idea,

00:20:53.560 --> 00:20:55.240
even if it's a
prototype or something.

00:20:55.240 --> 00:20:57.490
And they give you all the
stuff that you need in there

00:20:57.490 --> 00:20:59.770
to get started very quick.

00:20:59.770 --> 00:21:03.430
If you scale, then you
can almost graduate,

00:21:03.430 --> 00:21:07.090
cross that big bridge, to
the Google Cloud Platform

00:21:07.090 --> 00:21:10.810
to then start using all the
other bells and whistles,

00:21:10.810 --> 00:21:13.660
analytics and machine
learning and all that stuff.

00:21:13.660 --> 00:21:16.420
The difference that I've
seen, for example, on Cloud

00:21:16.420 --> 00:21:19.750
and all the other
products, specifically

00:21:19.750 --> 00:21:22.780
Android and YouTube, were
you draw this difference

00:21:22.780 --> 00:21:26.140
between the consumer world
and then the enterprise world.

00:21:26.140 --> 00:21:29.130
And I think we all
empathize with consumers.

00:21:29.130 --> 00:21:31.255
It's all the apps that
we use, Google Maps

00:21:31.255 --> 00:21:33.130
and everything we use today.

00:21:33.130 --> 00:21:35.830
And so it's kind
of, I think, easier

00:21:35.830 --> 00:21:39.550
to, as a cross-functional
team, design something

00:21:39.550 --> 00:21:40.870
that is easy to use.

00:21:40.870 --> 00:21:44.860
When you have PMs and engineers
and designers and researchers,

00:21:44.860 --> 00:21:47.200
the entire team is
all really focusing

00:21:47.200 --> 00:21:49.090
on making it super easy.

00:21:49.090 --> 00:21:51.910
With the enterprise world,
it's a little harder,

00:21:51.910 --> 00:21:55.180
because not everyone's going
to be using a machine learning

00:21:55.180 --> 00:21:57.220
model in Google Cloud.

00:21:57.220 --> 00:21:59.170
I don't know how to do that.

00:21:59.170 --> 00:22:02.860
So it starts getting
kind of tough.

00:22:02.860 --> 00:22:05.582
So there's a lot of
preconceptions, I feel like.

00:22:05.582 --> 00:22:07.540
How much information do
you need in the screen?

00:22:07.540 --> 00:22:11.530
Or is this OK for
a data scientist?

00:22:11.530 --> 00:22:13.030
There are things
that we don't know.

00:22:13.030 --> 00:22:16.710
It's still very, very early
for us to start figuring out

00:22:16.710 --> 00:22:21.620
how the consumer and
enterprise products should

00:22:21.620 --> 00:22:26.030
merge and should start
thinking, the data scientist

00:22:26.030 --> 00:22:29.210
is no different than my
mom that is using YouTube.

00:22:29.210 --> 00:22:30.290
We're just humans.

00:22:30.290 --> 00:22:34.550
How do we make it simple
and consistent for anyone?

00:22:34.550 --> 00:22:37.340
MARK MIRCHANDANI: In my
knowledge of enterprises,

00:22:37.340 --> 00:22:38.970
most of them have
people in them.

00:22:38.970 --> 00:22:40.280
[CHIME]

00:22:40.280 --> 00:22:42.580
And people tend to be the same.

00:22:42.580 --> 00:22:44.060
Maybe that's a bad assumption.

00:22:44.060 --> 00:22:46.520
I would have thought that the
design principles would apply

00:22:46.520 --> 00:22:48.890
equally, but I guess it
sounds like, as you're saying,

00:22:48.890 --> 00:22:50.390
there are a lot of
different factors

00:22:50.390 --> 00:22:53.000
to take into consideration
when designing these systems

00:22:53.000 --> 00:22:54.535
for almost different personas.

00:22:54.535 --> 00:22:55.910
MICHAEL KLEINERMAN:
That's right.

00:22:55.910 --> 00:22:57.680
GABI FERRARA: Is
there any resource

00:22:57.680 --> 00:23:01.120
you would like to share
about the job you do,

00:23:01.120 --> 00:23:05.750
if people want to get much
in-depth in the subject?

00:23:05.750 --> 00:23:07.110
You mentioned material design.

00:23:07.110 --> 00:23:09.300
I think it's one of the sources.

00:23:09.300 --> 00:23:12.380
But what people
should be looking for

00:23:12.380 --> 00:23:17.120
to make their product more
accessible and more efficient?

00:23:17.120 --> 00:23:20.510
Here, we have the guidelines,
as you mentioned, for Cloud,

00:23:20.510 --> 00:23:22.730
for YouTube, for Firebase.

00:23:22.730 --> 00:23:26.900
But in your opinion, where
can they get more in depth

00:23:26.900 --> 00:23:31.160
and adopt best practices--
would-be best practices,

00:23:31.160 --> 00:23:34.230
I think-- for product design?

00:23:34.230 --> 00:23:36.820
MICHAEL KLEINERMAN: I
think the material.io

00:23:36.820 --> 00:23:41.050
website is a really good place
to sort of stay up to date.

00:23:41.050 --> 00:23:45.330
They're updating that
website frequently and just

00:23:45.330 --> 00:23:51.780
adding all the new hotness that
my designers or even teams that

00:23:51.780 --> 00:23:55.360
don't have designers
should be learning from.

00:23:55.360 --> 00:23:58.120
So I think that's a
really good resource.

00:23:58.120 --> 00:24:02.460
I know other accessibility
sort of centralized websites

00:24:02.460 --> 00:24:05.222
within Google, but not entirely
sure outside of Google.

00:24:05.222 --> 00:24:06.180
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Yeah.

00:24:06.180 --> 00:24:08.946
I think it's interesting,
because there's no universal,

00:24:08.946 --> 00:24:11.023
this works for everybody design.

00:24:11.023 --> 00:24:12.690
There's no universal,
this kind of works

00:24:12.690 --> 00:24:14.095
for everybody accessibility.

00:24:14.095 --> 00:24:15.970
You said you worked at
a couple of companies,

00:24:15.970 --> 00:24:19.080
including Microsoft, and
then maybe a smaller agency

00:24:19.080 --> 00:24:20.555
before working at Google.

00:24:20.555 --> 00:24:22.680
What does that look like
when you go from one place

00:24:22.680 --> 00:24:23.640
to another?

00:24:23.640 --> 00:24:25.920
And how does that kind
of influence your thought

00:24:25.920 --> 00:24:28.230
about the design systems
that you're building?

00:24:28.230 --> 00:24:29.272
MICHAEL KLEINERMAN: Yeah.

00:24:29.272 --> 00:24:32.430
It's crazy how moving
to big corporations,

00:24:32.430 --> 00:24:34.020
you feel the difference.

00:24:34.020 --> 00:24:37.560
Also, at that point,
you're not just

00:24:37.560 --> 00:24:42.060
designing something and trying
to make it fit your needs.

00:24:42.060 --> 00:24:45.720
But you have to
start with trying

00:24:45.720 --> 00:24:50.580
to understand how to consume the
already-in-place design system.

00:24:50.580 --> 00:24:53.610
So that's one of the
goals of design systems.

00:24:53.610 --> 00:24:57.570
You're supposed to
build new things that

00:24:57.570 --> 00:25:00.870
are coherent with the rest
of the design language

00:25:00.870 --> 00:25:03.450
or family of components.

00:25:03.450 --> 00:25:05.880
That's definitely
a big difference

00:25:05.880 --> 00:25:09.420
that you would feel from
a startup or a place

00:25:09.420 --> 00:25:14.940
where you're still not there, in
terms of a clear plan to reuse

00:25:14.940 --> 00:25:16.620
components as much as possible.

00:25:16.620 --> 00:25:19.200
But you're just the
early phases of building.

00:25:19.200 --> 00:25:24.660
And I think building reusable
components comes much after.

00:25:24.660 --> 00:25:28.440
So it does feel quite different
when you join a big company,

00:25:28.440 --> 00:25:30.390
and then all those
reusable components

00:25:30.390 --> 00:25:31.518
are already in place.

00:25:31.518 --> 00:25:33.810
MARK MIRCHANDANI: What you
were talking about with kind

00:25:33.810 --> 00:25:36.290
of going to a new company
and joining all their assets,

00:25:36.290 --> 00:25:37.140
what we've seen--

00:25:37.140 --> 00:25:40.140
and this hearkens back to
you working at Microsoft,

00:25:40.140 --> 00:25:43.950
you talking about the Kinect,
because it was very interesting

00:25:43.950 --> 00:25:45.660
when the Kinect was out.

00:25:45.660 --> 00:25:47.510
For people who don't
know, the Kinect was--

00:25:47.510 --> 00:25:49.260
I'm not sure if it's
actually still around

00:25:49.260 --> 00:25:52.500
or if they're still working
on it as a separate tool.

00:25:52.500 --> 00:25:54.990
But it was a peripheral that
you could attach to your Xbox.

00:25:54.990 --> 00:25:57.540
And it would read you
with multiple cameras.

00:25:57.540 --> 00:26:00.060
And you could basically
interact with your Xbox

00:26:00.060 --> 00:26:03.060
through hand motions and
gestures and a microphone,

00:26:03.060 --> 00:26:03.790
as well.

00:26:03.790 --> 00:26:06.960
So it was a brand new paradigm
for interacting with your TV

00:26:06.960 --> 00:26:09.270
at that time, because most
people were playing games

00:26:09.270 --> 00:26:10.380
with controllers.

00:26:10.380 --> 00:26:15.300
And we saw a really interesting
kind of almost two interfaces

00:26:15.300 --> 00:26:19.080
mixing together when you had
your controller-level interface

00:26:19.080 --> 00:26:20.750
and then you had the
Kinect interface.

00:26:20.750 --> 00:26:23.400
And I think after the Kinect
was out for a little while,

00:26:23.400 --> 00:26:25.570
they kind of almost
merged those together.

00:26:25.570 --> 00:26:28.200
Or they tried to create an
interface that worked for both.

00:26:28.200 --> 00:26:30.510
And I had a Kinect,
and I had an Xbox.

00:26:30.510 --> 00:26:32.580
And I play a decent
amount of Xbox.

00:26:32.580 --> 00:26:34.080
I thought it was
really interesting,

00:26:34.080 --> 00:26:36.570
because some of the design
decisions in my opinion

00:26:36.570 --> 00:26:39.810
were actually worse on
the controller side,

00:26:39.810 --> 00:26:42.660
because they tried to
fold in the Kinect one.

00:26:42.660 --> 00:26:44.490
So that's a very
concrete example.

00:26:44.490 --> 00:26:47.670
But I guess in terms of
the long story there,

00:26:47.670 --> 00:26:50.650
the question is, how do you
make those kinds of decisions,

00:26:50.650 --> 00:26:54.758
especially when you're talking
about adding a new redesign?

00:26:54.758 --> 00:26:55.800
MICHAEL KLEINERMAN: Yeah.

00:26:55.800 --> 00:26:59.340
That's an interesting
story where we were just

00:26:59.340 --> 00:27:01.920
dealing with an unknown.

00:27:01.920 --> 00:27:07.320
No one had ever done
hand body gesture UIs.

00:27:07.320 --> 00:27:09.870
And there was nowhere
else to go and find

00:27:09.870 --> 00:27:13.890
references or inspiration
or research that

00:27:13.890 --> 00:27:16.960
was applicable to what
we were trying to do.

00:27:16.960 --> 00:27:19.080
So I think what
was happening also

00:27:19.080 --> 00:27:24.390
was hypotheses that it was the
best way for you to start using

00:27:24.390 --> 00:27:28.050
the UI, pick the game
or pick the movie

00:27:28.050 --> 00:27:30.150
or whatever you
were trying to do.

00:27:30.150 --> 00:27:33.630
And that way, you wouldn't
have to pick up the controller.

00:27:33.630 --> 00:27:36.300
And I think even the tagline
was "you are the controller"

00:27:36.300 --> 00:27:37.680
or something of that.

00:27:37.680 --> 00:27:40.140
And it turned out
that yeah, it was not

00:27:40.140 --> 00:27:45.480
nice to use your hands
to get to the content you

00:27:45.480 --> 00:27:47.140
were looking for.

00:27:47.140 --> 00:27:52.620
So it was better to use the
smarts of Kinect to play games

00:27:52.620 --> 00:27:54.180
because it was challenging.

00:27:54.180 --> 00:27:57.420
If you were playing, I
don't know, a tennis game,

00:27:57.420 --> 00:27:58.620
you're using your hands.

00:27:58.620 --> 00:28:01.500
And you were missing or losing.

00:28:01.500 --> 00:28:02.670
It was fun.

00:28:02.670 --> 00:28:04.560
It didn't matter,
because you were just

00:28:04.560 --> 00:28:06.970
excited to just
continue playing.

00:28:06.970 --> 00:28:08.760
But that same
mental model doesn't

00:28:08.760 --> 00:28:12.550
apply for you trying to find
a movie or a game in the UI.

00:28:12.550 --> 00:28:13.590
It's frustrating.

00:28:13.590 --> 00:28:16.470
So I think that's
when they deviated.

00:28:16.470 --> 00:28:19.080
I really don't know what the
answer is today, because I

00:28:19.080 --> 00:28:21.000
haven't played in a long time.

00:28:21.000 --> 00:28:25.080
But they deviated in when do you
use each controller for what.

00:28:25.080 --> 00:28:27.630
Or each input is good
for certain things.

00:28:27.630 --> 00:28:31.050
And how do we make sure that we
teach people when to use each?

00:28:31.050 --> 00:28:33.880
And what are the strengths of
each of the inputs that we're

00:28:33.880 --> 00:28:35.200
offering people?

00:28:35.200 --> 00:28:37.780
MARK MIRCHANDANI: I would
love to see a follow-up

00:28:37.780 --> 00:28:38.910
conversation.

00:28:38.910 --> 00:28:40.660
I'm assuming you still
have some contacts.

00:28:40.660 --> 00:28:43.630
But can we just get someone from
Microsoft, someone from Sony,

00:28:43.630 --> 00:28:45.370
someone from Nintendo?

00:28:45.370 --> 00:28:47.020
Can we all just
sit down in a room

00:28:47.020 --> 00:28:49.862
and hyper-analyze their
video game platforms

00:28:49.862 --> 00:28:50.945
and talk about the design?

00:28:50.945 --> 00:28:52.860
[LAUGHTER AND APPLAUSE]

00:28:52.860 --> 00:28:53.460
All right.

00:28:53.460 --> 00:28:55.430
B2, we're going to
leave that one to you.

00:28:55.430 --> 00:28:55.930
Awesome.

00:28:55.930 --> 00:28:57.340
Well, thanks so much
for joining us, Michael,

00:28:57.340 --> 00:28:59.860
and teaching us a lot about
kind of the thought process that

00:28:59.860 --> 00:29:00.687
goes into design.

00:29:00.687 --> 00:29:02.020
MICHAEL KLEINERMAN: My pleasure.

00:29:02.020 --> 00:29:02.565
Thank you.

00:29:02.565 --> 00:29:03.940
GABI FERRARA:
Thank you, Michael.

00:29:03.940 --> 00:29:06.040
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Very cool
talk with Michael-- super, super

00:29:06.040 --> 00:29:07.780
glad that he had a chance
to sit down with us

00:29:07.780 --> 00:29:09.030
and tell us a little bit more.

00:29:09.030 --> 00:29:10.300
I wouldn't have really known.

00:29:10.300 --> 00:29:12.820
Product designer-- it's
such an important part

00:29:12.820 --> 00:29:15.810
of what literally
everyone uses when

00:29:15.810 --> 00:29:17.060
they have to use the platform.

00:29:17.060 --> 00:29:18.160
So I think it's
really fascinating

00:29:18.160 --> 00:29:19.952
to see, look, there's
a lot of thought that

00:29:19.952 --> 00:29:21.220
goes into these decisions.

00:29:21.220 --> 00:29:24.400
GABI FERRARA: And responsibility
and implications, too,

00:29:24.400 --> 00:29:26.420
of decisions that people do.

00:29:26.420 --> 00:29:27.790
So that was very enlightening.

00:29:27.790 --> 00:29:30.040
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So in
another form of enlightenment,

00:29:30.040 --> 00:29:31.748
I'd love to ask the
question of the week.

00:29:31.748 --> 00:29:35.642
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:29:35.642 --> 00:29:37.100
So my question was
really about how

00:29:37.100 --> 00:29:39.530
to work with containers locally
and then kind of put them

00:29:39.530 --> 00:29:40.680
into the cloud.

00:29:40.680 --> 00:29:43.280
So we know that you can build
a container using Docker.

00:29:43.280 --> 00:29:44.120
And then you have to submit it.

00:29:44.120 --> 00:29:45.020
And then you have to pull it up.

00:29:45.020 --> 00:29:46.260
And then you use a build system.

00:29:46.260 --> 00:29:47.968
And then you have to
put it in the cloud.

00:29:47.968 --> 00:29:49.280
That seems pretty complicated.

00:29:49.280 --> 00:29:52.970
Could you kind of walk us
through what of those steps

00:29:52.970 --> 00:29:54.740
are necessary and
what makes sense?

00:29:54.740 --> 00:29:56.990
GABI FERRARA: So when I first
started with Kubernetes,

00:29:56.990 --> 00:29:59.720
for instance, I did not
know much about Docker.

00:29:59.720 --> 00:30:02.720
And in fact, it used to be a
thing that I loved to hate,

00:30:02.720 --> 00:30:05.520
because I didn't
understand how it worked.

00:30:05.520 --> 00:30:06.690
And now, I do.

00:30:06.690 --> 00:30:09.000
I think I understand
a bit how it works.

00:30:09.000 --> 00:30:12.650
And a lot of the menial
tasks I discovered

00:30:12.650 --> 00:30:17.150
can be really simple if
you're using the cloud.

00:30:17.150 --> 00:30:21.960
For instance, if you're running
a code locally and let's say

00:30:21.960 --> 00:30:25.220
you are in a place, you
don't have good internet,

00:30:25.220 --> 00:30:29.240
and you cannot do those Docker
pulls to download all those

00:30:29.240 --> 00:30:34.070
images, neither to push your
image to the cloud so you can

00:30:34.070 --> 00:30:37.940
deploy into Cloud Run or
Kubernetes, for instance,

00:30:37.940 --> 00:30:41.480
or even Compute Engine,
so what you can do--

00:30:41.480 --> 00:30:43.220
and it saves a lot of time.

00:30:43.220 --> 00:30:48.710
From your terminal, you can use
the Build command from G Cloud.

00:30:48.710 --> 00:30:54.600
And it says to submit the code
that's on the root folder.

00:30:54.600 --> 00:30:58.400
And once you do that, it's
going to do for you that Docker

00:30:58.400 --> 00:31:03.470
build, the Docker tag, the
Docker push, and in the end

00:31:03.470 --> 00:31:05.990
send it to Container Registry.

00:31:05.990 --> 00:31:09.080
It's going to be available
for all your projects on GCP--

00:31:09.080 --> 00:31:11.570
so a win-win situation.

00:31:11.570 --> 00:31:13.220
You don't use your internet.

00:31:13.220 --> 00:31:16.160
You just use Google's
internet to do it for you.

00:31:16.160 --> 00:31:18.230
And everything else is just fun.

00:31:18.230 --> 00:31:22.280
Then you just can deploy into
Kubernetes or Compute Engine

00:31:22.280 --> 00:31:26.070
or even on Cloud Run just
with simple command lines.

00:31:26.070 --> 00:31:28.850
So I think using
Cloud Build, it's

00:31:28.850 --> 00:31:31.670
going to save a lot
of development time

00:31:31.670 --> 00:31:33.982
on your day-to-day task.

00:31:33.982 --> 00:31:34.940
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Yeah.

00:31:34.940 --> 00:31:37.700
It sounds like a one-line
command that replaces probably

00:31:37.700 --> 00:31:40.550
five or six different
processes, all of which

00:31:40.550 --> 00:31:42.200
had their own
little nuances when

00:31:42.200 --> 00:31:44.870
you had to run them--
so super happy to see

00:31:44.870 --> 00:31:47.128
that it's a lot simpler than
hopefully it used to be.

00:31:47.128 --> 00:31:47.920
GABI FERRARA: Yeah.

00:31:47.920 --> 00:31:51.640
And you don't need to have that
much knowledge of Cloud Build.

00:31:51.640 --> 00:31:54.290
You don't need to
create fancy things.

00:31:54.290 --> 00:31:55.700
If you just want the basics--

00:31:55.700 --> 00:31:58.070
and usually, when you're
creating proof of concepts,

00:31:58.070 --> 00:31:59.750
that's just enough.

00:31:59.750 --> 00:32:01.460
And that helps a lot.

00:32:01.460 --> 00:32:04.370
If you want more, you can dig
into a bit on the documentation

00:32:04.370 --> 00:32:05.512
that's on the blog post.

00:32:05.512 --> 00:32:06.470
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Yeah.

00:32:06.470 --> 00:32:08.803
I think we've seen some pretty
cool customization to it,

00:32:08.803 --> 00:32:11.055
that you can set up really
complex build pipelines.

00:32:11.055 --> 00:32:13.430
But it sounds like even without
setting anything up, just

00:32:13.430 --> 00:32:15.260
the ability to speed
up container building

00:32:15.260 --> 00:32:16.478
and deploying is awesome.

00:32:16.478 --> 00:32:18.020
GABI FERRARA: I
agree, because nobody

00:32:18.020 --> 00:32:22.790
wants to stay there pushing an
image to the internet over 3G

00:32:22.790 --> 00:32:23.765
or airplane internet.

00:32:23.765 --> 00:32:26.390
MARK MIRCHANDANI: There are some
universal truths this episode,

00:32:26.390 --> 00:32:26.890
